From ffdebd79a204dcc5d2411ed9cae846ca23c4d008 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 13:05:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 58/60] Audio: at light load routine() waits until 32 samples
 are due before it renders (a minimum window)

Each render has a fixed cost besides its cost per sample: Song::renderAudio() and the FX go through every track and
kit, sounding or not. For tests/sdload's big project (S2: 64 synths, 14 kits, 30 audio tracks) with nothing playing
that is about 45k instructions a render, and routine() rendered whatever was due whenever it was called: about every
16 samples as the task manager schedules it (after the commit before last), which still left 57 % on the CPU
monitor.

Now, while the load is light, routine_() renders nothing when fewer than kMinSamplesDue = 32 samples are due, and
the audio task is made due when 32 will be (delayTask(), new in the task manager: it moves the task's lastCallTime,
which runTask() leaves alone after ignoreForStats()) instead of being called again after its 10 us back-off. Then it
renders, as always, twice what is due less 6: 60 samples, with 96 still in the output buffer. MIDI and gate output
waiting to go out (live, MIDI thru) is flushed on those calls too, as on a render.

Light load: cpuDireness at 0, no file being saved or loaded (minNumSamplesToRender has its own minimum then), and
windows of 32 samples or more rendered in under 50 % of their audio's time, measured around routine() (OS timer 0,
(7 * average + latest) / 8, so that one busy window where voices start doesn't switch it off). While that load keeps
it off, windows that long are only rendered when overloaded, so after 1/8 s without one it applies again and the
load starts afresh from the next. Windows of 64 gave underruns and QL in the emulator; 32 at up to 50 % leaves the
output buffer ~66 samples for a render that takes longer or for other tasks.

In the emulator (tests/sdload, CPU monitor, with the commits before): S2 idle 90 % -> 16 %, S3ref idle 76 % -> 10 %,
S4 playing (16 audio tracks streaming, a typical card) 93 % -> 38 %, without underruns, QL or culls; the audio
routine's calls (S2) 7,100/s -> 2,900/s, a quarter of them rendering. The cost: live input (MIDI in, pads) reaches the
audio with the next window, up to 60 samples (1.4 ms) later than before but with less queued after it (the latency
the same on average); the output buffer runs down to ~90 samples before a render instead of ~120, so a task that
blocks without yielding has 2.2 ms instead of ~2.8 ms before the audio underruns.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
